module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.Functor
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.DifferentialGraded.Module.DerivedTensorCounitDevissage
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.CompactObjects
public import StacksFrontier.Homology.DerivedCategory.LocallySmall
public import StacksFrontier.Homology.Perfect.LargeUniverse
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.MapTriangleHom

@[expose] public noncomputable section

/-!
# A morphism of exact direct-sum-preserving functors invertible on compact generators

Let `D` be a pre-triangulated category with direct sums which is generated by a family of compact
objects `E : ι → D`, and let `F, G : D ⥤ D'` be two exact functors which preserve those direct
sums. A morphism `τ : F ⟶ G` compatible with the shifts is then an isomorphism as soon as every
`τ.app (E i)` is one. This is the dévissage that extends an identification of two functors on a
generator to all of `D`, along the cell presentation of
`CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.exists_isHomotopyColimit_of_compactlyGenerated` ([stacks 09SN]).

The argument is the four closure properties of the class of objects where `τ` is invertible, and no
step needs more than `Pretriangulated`:

* isomorphisms, by naturality;
* shifts, by `CategoryTheory.NatTrans.app_shift`, which is where compatibility with the shift is
  spent;
* extensions in a distinguished triangle, by the five lemma
  `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.isIso₃_of_isIso₁₂` applied to `mapTriangleHom`, the morphism of
  triangles that `τ` induces;
* direct sums, by comparing the two colimit cocones that preservation supplies.

Derived colimits need nothing further: the defining triangle of a derived colimit has `∐ Kₙ` in its
first two places, so the direct-sum and extension steps already cover it. The cell presentation
then gives the conclusion by induction along the tower.

The intended application is `D = D(R)`, where the regular module in degree zero is a compact
generator. Two universe facts make that instantiation work, and together they pin the model of the
derived category: `CategoryTheory.IsCompactObject.{w}` is monotone increasing in `w`, and the cell
presentation tests compactness at the *hom* universe of `D`. The compactness of `R[0]` available
here, `StacksDraft.Perfect.isCompactObject_derived_single`, is compactness at the module universe
`u`. So the two agree exactly at a chosen derived category whose morphisms also live in `Type u`,
which is the hypothesis `[HasDerivedCategory.{u} (ModuleCat.{u} R)]` below — *not*
`HasDerivedCategory.standard`, whose morphisms live one level higher and for which the hypothesis
of the cell presentation cannot be met. Such a model exists, by
`StacksDraft.Perfect.AnyModel.nonempty_locallySmallDerivedCategory`.

## Main definitions

* `StacksDraft.CompactDevissage.mapTriangleHom` — the morphism of triangles induced by a morphism
  of functors compatible with the shift.
* `StacksDraft.CompactDevissage.natIsoOfIsCompactlyGenerated` — the natural isomorphism `F ≅ G`
  produced by the dévissage.

## Main results

* `StacksDraft.CompactDevissage.isIso_app_of_isCompactlyGenerated` and
  `StacksDraft.CompactDevissage.isIso_of_isCompactlyGenerated` — the dévissage itself.
* `StacksDraft.CompactDevissage.isIso_of_isIso_app_regularModule` — its form for `D(R)`: two exact
  functors out of `D(R)` which preserve direct sums are identified by any shift-compatible
  morphism between them that is invertible on the regular module in degree zero.

## References

* [The Stacks Project, Tag 09SN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09SN)
* [The Stacks Project, Tag 07LT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07LT)
-/

